Epistaxis
Opthalmology & ENT
Bright red nasal hemorrhage, Posterior pharyngeal blood drainage, Provocation by digital trauma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-020
Reason for Visit: Uncontrolled, continuous anterior epistaxis failing basic first aid and causing severe distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a spontaneous, continuous, high-volume anterior epistaxis lasting longer than 30 minutes, accompanied by posterior drainage and gagging in a hypertensive patient. This represents an acute airway and hemodynamic risk. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to secure the airway, achieve direct mechanical hemostasis, assess cardiovascular stability, and prevent an immediate hemorrhagic emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My nose started gushing blood half an hour ago, I can't get it to stop, and I'm choking on clots in the back of my throat."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a spontaneous, overt, bright red hemorrhage originating from the right anterior nasal cavity. The bleeding commenced 35 minutes ago, provoked by vigorous nose-blowing in a dry environment. The patient describes continuous blood draining down the posterior pharynx, leading to gagging and spitting up clots. Bleeding is strictly unilateral. Patient has a history of systemic hypertension and is highly anxious, which is exacerbating the bleeding rate. No history of bleeding disorders or anticoagulant use.
Functional Status: Sitting forward, highly distressed, continuously soaking through towels.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is panicked; patient is gagging on posterior drainage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/95 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Tachycardic, anxious, sitting leaning forward, holding a saturated towel to the face. Mild pallor.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the oropharynx reveals active bright red blood and small clots tracking down the posterior pharyngeal wall. Anterior rhinoscopy, after having the patient gently blow out large obstructing clots, reveals active, pulsatile bleeding from a prominent excoriated vessel in the anterior right nasal septum (Kiesselbach's plexus). Left nasal cavity is clear. No active bleeding from gums or other mucosal surfaces. Radial pulses are strong and bounding. Capillary refill is <2 seconds.
Environmental Observations: Heavily blood-soaked towels and tissues throughout the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes Perindopril for hypertension; compliant.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Unilateral Anterior Epistaxis - Status: Active Hemorrhage
Differentials:

Posterior Epistaxis (Sphenopalatine Artery) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visualization of the actively bleeding vessel in the anterior septum (Kiesselbach's plexus), and the bleeding is completely unilateral.

Coagulopathy/Bleeding Diathesis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of petechiae, purpura, easy bruising, bleeding from other mucosal surfaces, or history of anticoagulant medication.

Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear provocation by mechanical trauma (blowing) in the highly vascular anterior septum, lack of chronic unilateral hearing loss or neck masses, and immediate visible anterior source.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Anxiety driving severe hypertension and bleeding)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with uncontrolled, continuous anterior epistaxis complicated by posterior drainage and anxiety-driven severe hypertension. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical to prevent airway compromise from aspiration of clots and to definitively arrest the hemorrhage. Clinical examination successfully localized the bleeding to the anterior septum, ruling out a deeper, uncontrollable posterior bleed. By applying direct, sustained mechanical pressure and topical vasoconstriction, hemostasis was successfully achieved in the home, avoiding an emergency department transfer, stabilizing the patient's acute hypertensive response, and securing the airway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered topical oxymetazoline spray directly into the right nostril. Applied firm, continuous external digital pressure to the soft cartilaginous portion of the nose for exactly 15 minutes. Hemostasis successfully achieved. Cleaned the face and instructed the patient to remain seated and calm.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage future bleeds effectively, the patient must apply firm continuous pressure to the soft fleshy part of the nose and set an alarm for exactly 15 minutes, absolutely refusing to check if the bleeding has stopped before the alarm sounds. The explicit reward of a fully formed, stable clot upon release reinforces correct, sustained first-aid technique over the ineffective habit of intermittent dabbing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for formal blood pressure check and consideration of silver nitrate cautery if the vessel remains prominent.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if heavy bleeding restarts and does not stop after one strict 15-minute period of continuous pressure, or if the patient feels faint, dizzy, or begins vomiting large amounts of swallowed blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a burst blood vessel; the clot is fragile and prone to re-bleeding in the first 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The vessel has sealed, but the clot will take 3-5 days to fully stabilize into healing tissue. The high blood pressure was a reaction to the anxiety and should settle now that the bleeding has stopped.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the bleeding starts again, lean forward and pinch the soft part of your nose for 15 minutes without letting go. If it is still bleeding heavily after that, you must go to the emergency department.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A fragile blood vessel at the front of your nose burst from forceful blowing. We used pressure and medicine to clamp it shut and form a clot. You must protect this clot until it heals.
Actionable Steps: Do not blow your nose, pick your nose, or bend over to lift anything heavy for the next 48 hours. Sneeze with your mouth open to prevent pressure buildup in the nasal cavity.

